 ],
 "literal": "Those who admonish know nothing at all. / Like an egg of its mother, lying inside, / if it wastes away, other than that what does it possess? / Like the hatchling of a tortoise / is this love, if the lover leaves me helpless.",
 "translation": "Those who admonish me know nothing at all. My love is like the hatchling of a tortoise: just as its egg lies buried inside the sand and wastes away if deprived of its mother's presence, what else can my love do but perish if my lover leaves me helpless?",
 "modern": "அறிவுரை கூறுபவர்கள் எதையும் அறியாதவர்கள். தாயின் முட்டைக்குள் கிடந்து, அது அழிந்து போவதைத் தவிர வேறு என்ன பெருமையைப் பெறும்? காதலர் என்னைத் தவிக்கவிட்டுச் சென்றால், என் காதலும் ஆமைக்குஞ்சு போன்றதுதான்; அது தானாகவே அழிந்துவிடும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to her close friend or to those onlookers who criticize her for her deep distress during her lover's absence. She defends the depth of her feelings by comparing her love to a tortoise's egg and hatchling. According to classical Tamil poetic convention, tortoise hatchlings survive and grow only through the constant gaze or thoughts of their mother. If the lover abandons her, leaving her helpless, her love will languish and perish just like a hatchling deprived of its mother's life-giving attention. Thus, she explains that her grief is not a choice, but a natural consequence of being separated from the one who sustains her life.",
